{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"sorting","title":"Sorting & Custom Comparators","group":"STL Algorithms & Ranges","order":59,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>std::sort</code> is an introsort: O(n log n) worst case, not stable. Its relatives handle stability, partial results and selection.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sort-ascending\"></i><code>std::sort</code> and comparators</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;functional&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nstruct Employee { std::string name; int dept; int salary; };\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6};\n    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());                         // ascending\n    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), std::greater&lt;&gt;());       // descending\n    for (int x : v) std::cout &lt;&lt; x;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::vector&lt;std::string&gt; words{\"kiwi\", \"fig\", \"banana\", \"apple\"};\n    std::sort(words.begin(), words.end(), [](const auto&amp; a, const auto&amp; b) {\n        return a.size() &lt; b.size();                        // by length\n    });\n    for (const auto&amp; w : words) std::cout &lt;&lt; w &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::vector&lt;Employee&gt; staff{{\"ann\", 2, 70}, {\"bob\", 1, 50}, {\"cat\", 2, 90}, {\"dan\", 1, 60}};\n    std::sort(staff.begin(), staff.end(), [](const Employee&amp; a, const Employee&amp; b) {\n        if (a.dept != b.dept) return a.dept &lt; b.dept;      // dept ascending\n        return a.salary &gt; b.salary;                        // then salary descending\n    });\n    for (const auto&amp; e : staff) std::cout &lt;&lt; e.name &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>965521\nfig kiwi apple banana\ndan bob cat ann</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>A comparator must be a <strong>strict weak ordering</strong>: <code>comp(a, a)</code> must be <code>false</code>. Using <code>&lt;=</code> instead of <code>&lt;</code> breaks that and can make <code>std::sort</code> read out of bounds or crash.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stairs\"></i>Stable, partial, nth</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;std::pair&lt;int, std::string&gt;&gt; v{{2, \"b1\"}, {1, \"a\"}, {2, \"b2\"}, {1, \"c\"}};\n    std::stable_sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](const auto&amp; x, const auto&amp; y) { return x.first &lt; y.first; });\n    for (const auto&amp; [k, s] : v) std::cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; ' ';      // equal keys keep original order\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; scores{50, 90, 20, 70, 85, 60};\n    std::partial_sort(scores.begin(), scores.begin() + 3, scores.end(), std::greater&lt;&gt;());\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; scores[0] &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; scores[1] &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; scores[2] &lt;&lt; '\\n';     // top 3, sorted\n\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; m{7, 1, 5, 3, 9};\n    std::nth_element(m.begin(), m.begin() + 2, m.end());    // median in O(n) average\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; \"median \" &lt;&lt; m[2] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; p{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6};\n    auto mid = std::partition(p.begin(), p.end(), [](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; (mid - p.begin()) &lt;&lt; \" evens first\\n\";\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::is_sorted(p.begin(), p.end()) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>a c b1 b2\n90 85 70\nmedian 5\n3 evens first\n0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Result</th><th>Complexity</th><th>Stable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>sort</code></td><td>Fully sorted</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>stable_sort</code></td><td>Fully sorted, equal elements keep order</td><td>O(n log n) (O(n log² n) without extra memory)</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>partial_sort(first, mid, last)</code></td><td>Smallest <code>mid-first</code> elements sorted at the front</td><td>O(n log k)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>nth_element</code></td><td>Element at <em>n</em> is what it would be if sorted; smaller before, larger after</td><td>O(n) avg</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>partition</code> / <code>stable_partition</code></td><td>Predicate-true elements first</td><td>O(n)</td><td>No / Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>list::sort()</code></td><td>For <code>std::list</code> (no random access)</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-numbers\"></i>Sort indices, not objects</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;numeric&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; height{170, 155, 182, 160};\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; idx(height.size());\n    std::iota(idx.begin(), idx.end(), 0);                       // 0 1 2 3\n    std::sort(idx.begin(), idx.end(), [&amp;](int a, int b) { return height[a] &lt; height[b]; });\n    for (int i : idx) std::cout &lt;&lt; i &lt;&lt; ' ';                    // argsort\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 3 0 2</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What algorithm does std::sort use?</summary><div><p><em>Introsort</em>: quicksort with median-of-three pivots, switching to heapsort if the recursion gets too deep (guaranteeing O(n log n) worst case) and to insertion sort for small partitions. It's not stable; use <code>std::stable_sort</code> (a merge sort) when the order of equal elements matters.</p></div></details></section>"}